Report operating-system security checks beside the firmware ones.

The shield in Privacy & Security opens a dialog that says "Protected" or
"Security Checks Failed" and offers to copy a technical report. Everything it
knows comes from fwupd's GetHostSecurityAttrs, which is an index of *firmware*
protections: Secure Boot, the SPI descriptor, Intel BootGuard, TPM
reconstruction. That is real, and it is half the question. A Linux desktop is
also secure or not according to whether its disk is encrypted, whether the
kernel is locked down, whether modules are signed, whether the IOMMU is on and
whether the usual hardening sysctls are set -- none of which fwupd reports
except where a plugin happens to exist, and none of which appear here at all.

Worse, the page hides itself entirely on a virtual machine and on any chassis
fwupd cannot identify, because a firmware security index means nothing there.
The operating-system half still does.

So this adds a second provider. io.losos.Security1 answers GetHostSecurityAttrs
in the same a{sv} shape fwupd uses -- deliberately, so this patch is "ask a
second bus name and file the answers separately" rather than a second parser
and a second widget. Its attributes go to their own table, not into the HSI
tables: HSI levels are defined by fwupd for firmware, and filing "disk
encryption" under HSI-1 would claim an equivalence that does not exist.

The dialog then lists the checks. Upstream keeps them inside the copied report
only, which is defensible while every check is about firmware the reader cannot
change from here; it stops being defensible once the list includes a lockdown
mode and four sysctls, which can be fixed by someone who is told which one
failed. Each check becomes an expandable row -- title, outcome, and the
provider's description with the path it read.

Two ordering details are load-bearing. The two providers reply in either order,
so neither callback can decide on its own that the panel is unavailable; that
is now a count of how many have answered and how many had anything to say, and
the "unavailable" page appears only when both have failed. And the fwupd proxy
failure path now counts itself, which upstream did not need to do: without it a
machine with the second provider and no fwupd waits forever for a verdict.

Not upstreamable as-is -- it hardcodes one vendor's bus name. Upstream would
want a list of providers, which is the shape this leaves room for.
